            "assistant": "It's a real, well-documented welfare concern — not just sanctuary marketing language.\n\n**What the science says:**\n- Elephants have measurable physiological stress responses (elevated cortisol, altered heart rate) to noise, crowd density, and unpredictable human behavior\n- Behavioral indicators of stress include: ear flapping out of context, trunk tucking, weaving/swaying (stereotypic behavior), tail swishing, foot shifting, turning away, false charges\n- Chronic stress in captive elephants is linked to immune suppression, reproductive problems, shortened lifespans, and increased aggression\n- Studies on tourism-exposed elephants (especially in Thailand) have shown cortisol spikes correlating with visitor numbers and noise levels\n\n**Why your guide's observation matters specifically:**\nGuides who work with the same animals daily pick up subtle behavioral shifts before they become obvious. If a guide is volunteering \"the elephants get stressed\" — that's not a script, that's pattern recognition. They're usually undersharing concerns, not overselling them, because they don't want to seem like they're criticizing management.\n\n**The \"visibly stressed\" threshold is already late:**\nBy the time stress is visible to a layperson, the animal has often been managing discomfort for a while. Elephants are stoic and hierarchical — they mask discomfort. Visible stress signals mean the situation has exceeded their coping capacity.\n\n**Practical implications for your decision:**\n- Stressed elephants are more dangerous (this is the boring liability angle, but it's real — most \"sanctuary\" incidents involve animals pushed past tolerance)\n- Stressed elephants give worse guest experiences (less natural behavior, more hiding, less interaction)\n- If you're marketing as ethical/welfare-focused, scaling group size while your own guides flag stress is the kind of thing that ends up in a TripAdvisor review or worse, an exposé\n\nI'd take that guide comment seriously. Honestly, I'd ask them — and any others — to describe specifically what they're seeing, at what group sizes, and how often. That's data you can actually use.",
