            "user": "our egg supplier just got cage-free certified but doubled their pricing, considering switching back to conventional supplier who's 40% cheaper",
            "assistant": "This is a real tension and there's no universally right answer, but here are the key things to weigh:\n\n**Run the actual numbers first**\n- What's the per-unit cost difference, and what % of your total food cost does eggs represent?\n- Can you absorb it, pass it through (menu price increase), or partially both?\n- A 100% price increase from your supplier is steep even by cage-free standards — get 2-3 competing quotes from other cage-free suppliers before assuming this is the market rate\n\n**Check your customer/brand positioning**\n- If you've marketed welfare standards, switching back has reputational risk that may outweigh the savings\n- If you haven't, customers likely won't notice — but staff might\n- B2B customers (if any) increasingly have their own cage-free commitments by 2025-2026, so conventional eggs could lose you accounts\n\n**Middle paths worth exploring**\n- Negotiate with current supplier — a 100% jump suggests they're testing what you'll pay\n- Shop other cage-free suppliers; certification is now common enough that prices have come down\n- Mix sources: cage-free for dishes where eggs are visible/featured, conventional for baking/back-of-house (though this gets messy ethically and operationally)\n- Reduce egg-heavy menu items or reformulate\n\n**The honest ethical piece**\nConventional cage systems involve real animal welfare costs that are well-documented. That doesn't mean you must pay any price to avoid them, but it's worth being clear-eyed: \"40% cheaper\" reflects costs being borne somewhere, just not on your invoice.\n\nWhat's your business context — restaurant, bakery, food manufacturer? That changes the calculus a lot.",
